      </ol>
    </nav>

    <h2 id="sec-1">Where we are and what that means</h2>
    <p>The shop sits at 112 Nguyen Van Cu in Long Bien, the district on the Red River's north bank, which places it just across the river from the Old Quarter and gives the first-time visitor two practical gifts: a real neighborhood to learn the bike in, and the Long Bien Bridge, Hanoi's most storied crossing, as the direct route into the historic center. The area guides on this site cover the river crossings and the Old Quarter streets in detail, including the <a href="/motorbike-rental-hanoi-old-quarter/long-bien-bridge-motorbike-guide-hanoi/">Long Bien Bridge riding guide</a>.</p>
    <p>What the location means for planning is simple: your first kilometers happen in Long Bien's calmer streets, where a new rider can find the throttle and the brakes without the Old Quarter's full texture, and the crossing to the center arrives as a deliberate second act rather than an immediate plunge. First-time visitors who use that geography well start their week measurably calmer.</p>
    <p>The practical geography note for planning: Nguyen Van Cu is a main artery of the Long Bien side, easy to reach from the east of the city, and connected toward the center by the bridge approaches. Study the route from your accommodation before pickup day, per the route-planning article in this series, and the visit itself becomes the easiest errand of your trip.</p>

    <h2 id="sec-2">When to come: timing the pickup</h2>
    <p>The shop is open 09:00&ndash;21:00 daily, and the honest timing advice is to avoid the edges of that window. A morning pickup gives you a full day of daylight for the first ride and the widest margin for questions, adjustments or a machine swap if the fit is not right; a pickup an hour before close compresses the handover and leaves the first ride for tomorrow anyway. Aim for the hours when neither side is rushing.</p>
    <p>Avoid rush-hour arrivals if your route to us passes through the bridge corridors, because the approaches carry the city's heaviest flows at the working day's hinges, and a first-time visitor's arrival is calmer outside those peaks. The seasonal article in this series covers weather considerations, and the general climate planning is on the <a href="/motorbike-guides/best-time-to-ride-motorbike-hanoi/">best-time-to-ride guide</a>; pick a dry-window morning in the rainy season and the whole day improves.</p>
    <p>Booking ahead ties the timing together. A call or message before pickup day, to <a href="tel:+84942467674">0942 467 674</a> or nguyentuantu8x@gmail.com, confirms the machine you want, the helmets, and the rough hour, so the visit is a handover rather than a negotiation. The rental-paperwork article lists the questions worth asking in the same call.</p>

    <h2 id="sec-3">What to bring and what to leave behind</h2>
    <p>Bring the essentials: your identification, the license questions resolved beforehand per the <a href="/renting-a-motorbike-in-vietnam/motorbike-license-vietnam-tourists/">tourist license guide</a>, the deposit in the agreed shape, and your plans, honestly stated, so the machine matches the week. Bring your own helmet if you ride with one at home; it is never required, but riders who own a good one enjoy having it, and the <a href="/motorbike-guides/motorbike-helmet-guide-hanoi/">helmet guide</a> covers the fit reasoning that applies to rentals and owners alike.</p>
    <p>Leave behind what does not fit on a motorbike: large suitcases, breakables and the whole category of luggage that turns a bike into a pack animal. The storage logic on the bike-choice article in this series applies doubly on pickup day; settle at your accommodation first, come to us light, and the machine rides like a rental instead of a moving van.</p>
    <p>Bring weather sense as well as gear. In the rainy season, a dry-window plan and a light rain layer beat optimism, per the rainy-day article in this series; in the cool months, layers that work under a jacket. What you wear to the pickup is what you ride in, so dress for the bike, not for the counter.</p>

    <h2 id="sec-4">The first ride: from Long Bien into the city</h2>
    <p>Plan the first ride as its own event, in three stages. Stage one: the quiet loop, five unhurried minutes in the streets around the shop, testing throttle, brakes and balance with the inspection article's checklist still fresh. Stage two: the bridge crossing, riding the Long Bien route toward the center at a deliberate pace, absorbing the river view without hurrying the traffic. Stage three: a short, planned circuit on the center side, the Hoan Kiem loop described on the <a href="/motorbike-rental-hanoi-old-quarter/hoan-kiem-lake-riding-hanoi/">Hoan Kiem riding guide</a>, before returning the same way.</p>
    <p>First-time visitors should keep the first day's ambitions low and the day's route known. The traffic-preparation article in this series covers the street-level habits for Hanoi's flow, and the route-planning article builds the progression from neighborhood loops to the wider city over the week's first days. The geometry of a Long Bien pickup makes that progression unusually natural: calm streets, then the bridge, then the center, each stage arriving with the last one mastered.</p>
    <p>One honest note on the bridge itself: the Long Bien crossing is a riding experience with its own surface and traffic character, described candidly on its guide on this site, and the first-time visitor's approach is steady speed, generous distance from the vehicle ahead and no photography while moving. The views deserve stopping for, and stopping for them is the correct way to have them.</p>

    <h2 id="sec-5">Planning the machine handover</h2>
    <p>The handover is where the week's quality gets decided, so give it ten unhurried minutes rather than a signature and a key. Walk the inspection from the pre-ride article with us present, agree the condition with photos, try the helmet's fit, and set the mirrors for your posture. Ask the support and fuel questions from the paperwork article, and leave knowing where everything is: fuel, locks, the phone number that answers.</p>
    <p>If the machine does not feel right, say so immediately, in the shop's light, where a swap is a conversation rather than a logistical adventure. The bike-choice article explains the tier logic, budget automatics at a verified 150,000 VND per day, premium automatics at 200,000 VND per day, with the full verified table on the <a href="/hanoi-motorbike-rental-cost/">cost hub</a>, and an honest word at handover beats a week of quiet regret about a machine one size wrong.</p>
    <p>Leave with a plan we both share: the week's rough shape, any trip intentions beyond the city, the expected return day and time, and the way home from our door. The return-day article in this series covers the end of the rental in the same spirit this page covers its beginning, and rentals that begin this way end the same way: quickly, calmly and without surprises.</p>

    <h2 id="sec-6">If plans change before or after pickup</h2>
    <p>Plans flex, and the time to say so is as early as the flex appears. A delayed arrival, a machine preference change, an extra day needed at the end: each is a normal part of rental life and each is easiest as an early message to the number that already answered your booking. First-time visitors sometimes hesitate to report small changes as if they were impositions; the opposite is true, and a shop that knows your week's actual shape can serve it better.</p>
    <p>If the trip's scope changes materially, a day trip added, a highland route considered, that conversation belongs at the shop rather than in an article, because the machine should match the trip's hardest day. The trip-planning guidance on the <a href="/hanoi-motorbike-trips/long-distance-motorbike-trip-planning/">long-distance planning guide</a> frames that decision honestly, and the machine-side answer is a swap to a premium automatic if the plan outgrew the budget tier, per the bike-choice article.</p>
    <p>And if something goes wrong mid-rental, from a mechanical feeling to a schedule collapse, the same early-call rule applies doubly. Support during the rental is part of what a rental from an established shop includes, and the quiet confidence of knowing that is part of what you take from the pickup-day conversation.</p>

    <h2 id="sec-7">The pickup-day checklist</h2>
    <p>Condensed, the day looks like this. Before: book by call or message, resolve the license questions, study the route from your accommodation, and pick a dry-window morning inside 09:00&ndash;21:00. Bring: identification, the agreed deposit, weather-appropriate layers, small luggage only, and your own helmet if you have one. At the shop: inspect with us present, photograph the condition, fit the helmet, set the mirrors, ask the paperwork questions, and agree the week's shape and the return day.</p>
    <p>Then: the quiet loop, the bridge, the first short circuit, and home by a route you know. Nothing on that list takes more than minutes, and together they are the difference between a rental that starts at the counter and a rental that starts on the road.</p>
    <p>To set the day up, call <a href="tel:+84942467674">0942 467 674</a>, email <a href="mailto:nguyentuantu8x@gmail.com">nguyentuantu8x@gmail.com</a> or visit 112 Nguyen Van Cu, Long Bien, Hanoi, open 09:00&ndash;21:00 daily. Verified prices start from 150,000 VND per day, the fleet and rates are on the cost hub, and the first-time visitor who plans the pickup this way starts the week the way the best weeks start: unhurried.</p>

    <h2>Frequently asked questions about pickup planning</h2>
    <article class="faq-entry">
      <h2>Where do I pick up a rental motorbike in Hanoi?</h2>
      <p>At our shop at 112 Nguyen Van Cu, Long Bien, Hanoi, open 09:00&ndash;21:00 daily, just across the Red River from the Old Quarter with the Long Bien crossing as the direct route to the center. Book ahead by call or email to confirm the machine and the rough hour, and study the route from your accommodation before pickup day.</p>
    </article>
    <article class="faq-entry">
      <h2>What is the best time of day to pick up a rental motorbike?</h2>
      <p>A morning inside our opening hours, outside the rush-hour peaks, in a dry window if the season allows it. A morning handover leaves the full day for the first ride, widest margin for questions or a machine swap, and time for the quiet first loop before the bridge and the center. Avoid arriving in the last hour before close.</p>
    </article>
    <article class="faq-entry">
      <h2>Can the rental motorbike be delivered to my hotel?</h2>
      <p>Delivery arrangements, where they exist, are confirmed directly with us at booking rather than assumed from anything written here. The standard plan is a pickup at the Long Bien shop, which doubles as a calm place to learn the machine before riding into the city, and most first-time visitors find the visit worth the trip for that alone.</p>
    </article>

